WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.960
Every other second messenger you have met is built, and then broken.

2
00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:13.020
One enzyme makes cyclic AMP, and another enzyme destroys it.

3
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.200
Calcium is an element.

4
00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:18.780
No enzyme can make it, and none can break it down.

5
00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:22.040
So how can a bare ion carry a message?

6
00:00:22.933 --> 00:00:26.653
The cell moves calcium out, and keeps it out.

7
00:00:27.353 --> 00:00:33.193
A resting cell holds free calcium in its cytosol, the main fluid, near 100

8
00:00:33.193 --> 00:00:41.333
nanomolar. Outside, the level is 1 to 2 millimolar, 10,000 to 20,000 times higher.

9
00:00:42.433 --> 00:00:47.693
The endoplasmic reticulum is a folded compartment that serves as the calcium store.

10
00:00:48.373 --> 00:00:51.613
There the free level is several hundred micromolar.

11
00:00:51.613 --> 00:00:52.450
. . . .

12
00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:59.530
Think of a dam. The reservoir is held high at a steady cost, and opening one gate

13
00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:01.230
gives a flood at once.

14
00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:04.970
To send a calcium signal, the cell makes nothing.

15
00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:10.670
It opens a channel, and ions already pressed against the far side come through

16
00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:11.670
within milliseconds.

17
00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:18.290
The starting level is so low that a modest number of ions raises it tenfold, to

18
00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:19.470
about one micromolar.

19
00:01:20.283 --> 00:01:23.703
Every channel has a pump against it.

20
00:01:24.203 --> 00:01:28.283
A channel only opens a path, and the gradient does the work.

21
00:01:29.103 --> 00:01:35.143
When it closes, a pump spends energy to take calcium out of the cytosol, and that

22
00:01:35.143 --> 00:01:42.003
ends the signal. The pump called SERCA moves two calcium ions into the store for

23
00:01:42.003 --> 00:01:47.383
each ATP. Buffer proteins bind calcium and take it out of play.

24
00:01:48.167 --> 00:01:52.807
A protein senses calcium with a pocket lined with oxygens.

25
00:01:53.807 --> 00:02:00.147
Calmodulin is a small protein with four of these pockets, called EF hands, two in

26
00:02:00.147 --> 00:02:01.587
each of two lobes.

27
00:02:02.387 --> 00:02:08.727
When calcium binds, the lobes open, then close around a helix on a target, like two

28
00:02:08.727 --> 00:02:09.927
hands gripping a rope.

29
00:02:10.757 --> 00:02:17.147
In the enzyme CaM kinase II, a segment lies across the active site and blocks it.

30
00:02:17.947 --> 00:02:21.227
Calmodulin grips beside that segment and pulls it clear.

31
00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:26.220
A rise in an ion has become a phosphate on a protein.

32
00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:29.160
The cell made nothing to start it.

33
00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:32.620
Cyclic AMP is made and then broken.

34
00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:37.540
A channel lets calcium in, and a pump takes it out again.

35
00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:44.440
So keep this rule: calcium cannot be made or destroyed, so the cell moves it.
